-- title:
--   Primes below the node ideal are the two branch centres
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $0$, $A\subseteq L$ a valuation subring with $A\neq L$ in which, for $a$ in the maximal ideal and $b\neq 0$, some power $a^n$ is divisible by $b$, and let $F$ be a field with an $L$-algebra structure. Let $X$ be an integral scheme with a proper, flat, locally finitely presented morphism $\mathrm{toBase}\colon X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ all of whose stalks are integrally closed, and let $\varphi\colon F\simeq$ the function field of $X$ be a ring isomorphism carrying each $a\in A$ to its image under `SemistableModel.baseToFunctionField`; for a point $\eta$ write $\mathcal O_\eta$ for the subring $\varphi^{-1}(\text{image of the stalk at }\eta)$ of $F$. Assumed: points $\eta_i\neq\eta_j$ and $x$ over the closed point of $A$, with $x$ closed, $x$ outside the smooth locus, $x$ in the closure of each of $\eta_i,\eta_j$, both $\eta_i,\eta_j$ non‑closed, every non‑closed point specialising to $x$ over the closed point equal to $\eta_i$ or $\eta_j$, $x$ the unique closed point over the closed point lying in both closures, and $\mathcal O_\eta$ a valuation subring of $F$ for every non‑closed $\eta$ over the closed point. Further assumed, summarised here: a constant layer $C'\subseteq A$ which is a discrete valuation ring with $C'\cap\mathfrak m_A=\varpi'C'$ and $A$ algebraic over it; a local Noetherian subring $N_0\subseteq\mathcal O_{\eta_i}\cap\mathcal O_{\eta_j}$ containing $C'$, with residues and $C'$-independence conditions; node coordinates $x_N,y_N,u_N\in N_0$ with $u_N$ a unit, $x_Ny_N=\varpi_0^{E_0}u_N$ for $\varpi_0\in C'\setminus\{0\}$ with $\varpi_0^{E_0}\in\mathfrak m_A$, and $x_N$ a non‑unit of $\mathcal O_{\eta_i}$ but a unit of $\mathcal O_{\eta_j}$ and $y_N$ conversely; an isomorphism of the $\mathfrak m$-adic completion of $N_0$ with $W[[U,V]]/(UV-\pi^E)$ over a complete discrete valuation ring $W$, $E\ge 1$, matching constants and sending $\pi$ to $\varpi'$; the subring $T$ generated by $N_0$ and the image of $A$, with $F$ its fraction field; the ideal $\mathfrak p$ of $T$ spanned by the elements of $T$ that are non‑units of $N_0$ together with those that are images of elements of $\mathfrak m_A$; a subring $B\subseteq N_0$ containing $x_N,y_N,u_N$, generated by the image of $C'$ and a finite set, with $N_0$ its localisation; and the hypothesis that any non‑closed $\eta$ over the closed point with $B\subseteq\mathcal O_\eta$ admits an element of $B$ which is a non‑unit of $N_0$ and a unit of $\mathcal O_\eta$. The conclusion: every prime ideal $\mathfrak q$ of $T$ containing the images of all elements of $\mathfrak m_A$ that lie in $T$ and satisfying $\mathfrak q\subsetneq\mathfrak p$ consists exactly of the $t\in T$ that are not invertible in $\mathcal O_{\eta_i}$, or exactly of the $t\in T$ that are not invertible in $\mathcal O_{\eta_j}$.
--
--   This is the "two branches" step in the recognition of a node on an arithmetic surface: inside the base-extended node ring $T=N_0\cdot A$ the primes over $\mathfrak m_A$ lying strictly below the node ideal are precisely the two centres in $T$ of the maximal ideals of the local rings at the two generic points through the node. It is used in [`AlgebraicGeometry.mem_localRing_node_iff_exists_mul_eq_of_nodeChart_of_forall_not_dominates`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.mem_localRing_node_iff_exists_mul_eq_of_nodeChart_of_forall_not_dominates), where the local rings at $\eta_i,\eta_j$ are identified from the crossing chart.
